    const svgInstructions=`Create a reusable, purpose-specific Qanvas Manual SVG—not a generic badge used only to satisfy an SVG requirement. Return exactly one standalone <svg> element with xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" and a viewBox. Prefer a native Qanvas object or the timelineNode, callout, or arrowLabel template when it already expresses the requested design; use Manual SVG for genuinely custom geometry.\n\nMatch the requested visual semantics. A timeline must visibly contain an axis/path, ticks or nodes, and readable date/period labels. A process graphic must show meaningful direction and grouping. Do not replace a requested timeline with a rounded background capsule, an unchanged default template, or unrelated decorative artwork. Separate native graphicText cards may be used for event narratives when they are positioned and connected to the real axis.\n\nFor every editable <text> or leaf <tspan>, add data-qanvas-field="stable-name" and data-qanvas-label="Human label". Example: <text data-qanvas-field="headline" data-qanvas-label="Headline" font-size="28">Title</text>. Add the same attributes to editable graphic elements such as rect, path, circle, polygon, or line to expose fill, stroke, and stroke-width. Use data-qanvas-edit="background" only on the real background shape.\n\nUse {{theme:accent}}, {{theme:surface}}, {{theme:text}}, {{theme:grid}}, or another theme role for colors whenever possible. Direct bindings may be used in text or safe attributes: {{parameter}}, {{measure:name}}, {{field:name}}, and {{theme:role}}. Text wrapped in a safe <a href="https://example.com"> exposes that link in the SVG text editor.\n\nKeep text readable at the final rendered size. Prefer a plain fill with no text stroke. If a contrast outline is necessary, keep stroke-width at or below 10% of font-size (for example at most 1 for font-size 10); a thick paint-order="stroke" halo can swallow the light fill and make labels look dark. Preview labels against the resolved page 